# vendor/firn/patches/0007-time-wait-slot-reuse.patch
#
# ROUND ROADMAP-5, K-008 (Osum, 24.09.2026) -- A FULL CONNECTION TABLE
# HANDS OVER ITS OLDEST TIME_WAIT SLOT.
#
# WHY. The stack has MAXCONN = 8 slots, and the side that closes first
# keeps its slot for TIME_WAIT_US = 60 s. `drucke` asks the printer
# every half second whether the job is done, one `Connection: close`
# request per question -- after eight connections the table was full,
# every further connect failed at once, and a job the printer had
# finished after 14 s was reported after 60 s (measured: CUPS'
# ippeveprinter said `completed` at 14 s, drucke saw it at 60 s; the
# serial log shows the connects failing from the 8th connection on
# until the first TIME_WAIT ran out). The same wall stands in front of
# every short-connection client: HTTP without keep-alive, DNS over TCP.
#
# WHAT. `tcp_free_slot` takes a free slot as before. Only when there is
# none, it takes the TIME_WAIT slot closest to running out, clears it
# and counts ST_TW_REUSE. A connection in TIME_WAIT has no owner any
# more; the guard protects the SAME four-tuple against old segments
# (RFC 9293 3.6.1), and the new connection gets a fresh ISS -- the
# condition under which RFC 1122 4.2.2.13 / RFC 6191 let TIME_WAIT end
# early. Linux does the same when tcp_max_tw_buckets overflows.
#
# WHY A PATCH AND NOT FIRN: see 0006 -- the pin stays the pin; the
# change belongs to Firn in the long run (REMOVE-FROM-FIRN.md).
#
